AWS EC2 TO GITHUB USING GIT AND SSH

Repository:
https://github.com/ksumathi-senthil/aws-docker-project

OBJECTIVE

Connect an AWS EC2 Linux server to GitHub using Git and SSH.

The workflow is:

AWS EC2
|
| Git + SSH
v
GitHub Repository
|
v
aws-docker-project

CHECK CURRENT LOCATION

Check current user:

whoami

Check current directory:

pwd

List files:

ls -ltra

Example:

/root
README.md
Docker
yes
yes.pub
.ssh/

IMPORTANT:
Do NOT push the entire /root directory to GitHub.

Files such as "yes" may be private SSH keys.

CREATE PROJECT DIRECTORY

Create a dedicated project directory:

mkdir -p /root/aws-docker-project

Go into the project:

cd /root/aws-docker-project

Check:

pwd

Expected:

/root/aws-docker-project

COPY PROJECT FILES

Copy only files that belong to the project.

Example:

cp /root/README.md /root/aws-docker-project/
cp /root/Docker /root/aws-docker-project/

Check:

ls -la

INITIALIZE GIT

Inside the project directory:

cd /root/aws-docker-project

Initialize Git:

git init

This creates:

.git

Verify:

ls -la

CONFIGURE GIT USER

Set Git username:

git config --global user.name "ksumathi-senthil"

Set GitHub email:

git config --global user.email "YOUR_GITHUB_EMAIL"

Check configuration:

git config --global --list

CREATE .gitignore

Create the file:

nano .gitignore

Add:

SSH private keys

*.pem
*_ed25519
*_rsa
id_rsa
id_ed25519

Environment files

.env
.env.*

Logs

*.log

OS files

.DS_Store

Save the file.

CHECK FILES BEFORE ADDING

Run:

git status

Make sure only project files are being added.

DO NOT add:

Private SSH keys
Passwords
API keys
AWS credentials
.env files
.pem files

ADD FILES TO GIT

Run:

git add .

Check:

git status

CREATE INITIAL COMMIT

Run:

git commit -m "Initial commit"

CHANGE BRANCH TO MAIN

Run:

git branch -M main

Check:

git branch

Expected:

main
CREATE SSH KEY FOR GITHUB

Create an ED25519 SSH key:

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "YOUR_GITHUB_EMAIL" -f /root/github_ed25519

This creates:

/root/github_ed25519
/root/github_ed25519.pub

IMPORTANT:

github_ed25519
= PRIVATE KEY

github_ed25519.pub
= PUBLIC KEY

NEVER SHARE:

/root/github_ed25519

The private key must remain secret.

DISPLAY PUBLIC KEY

Run:

cat /root/github_ed25519.pub

Copy the entire output.

It will look similar to:

ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3... YOUR_GITHUB_EMAIL

ADD SSH KEY TO GITHUB

Log in to GitHub as:

ksumathi-senthil

Go to:

GitHub
-> Settings
-> SSH and GPG keys
-> New SSH key

Title:

AWS EC2

Key type:

Authentication Key

Paste the complete contents of:

/root/github_ed25519.pub

Click:

Add SSH key

CONFIGURE SSH

Create SSH configuration:

nano /root/.ssh/config

Add:

Host github.com
HostName github.com
User git
IdentityFile /root/github_ed25519
IdentitiesOnly yes

Save the file.

SET SSH FILE PERMISSIONS

Run:

chmod 600 /root/.ssh/config

chmod 600 /root/github_ed25519

chmod 644 /root/github_ed25519.pub

TEST GITHUB SSH AUTHENTICATION

Run:

ssh -T git@github.com

Successful output:

Hi ksumathi-senthil! You've successfully authenticated, but GitHub does not provide shell access.

This is NORMAL.

It means SSH authentication is working.

CONFIGURE GITHUB REMOTE

Go to the project:

cd /root/aws-docker-project

Add GitHub remote:

git remote add origin git@github.com:ksumathi-senthil/aws-docker-project.git

Check:

git remote -v

Expected:

origin git@github.com:ksumathi-senthil/aws-docker-project.git (fetch)
origin git@github.com:ksumathi-senthil/aws-docker-project.git (push)

IF ORIGIN ALREADY EXISTS

If you see:

error: remote origin already exists.

Check:

git remote -v

If the URL is incorrect:

git remote set-url origin git@github.com:ksumathi-senthil/aws-docker-project.git

Check again:

git remote -v

PUSH PROJECT TO GITHUB

Run:

cd /root/aws-docker-project

git push -u origin main

Successful output should contain something similar to:

main -> main

After the first successful push, future pushes can normally use:

git push

NORMAL DAILY GIT WORKFLOW

Every time you make project changes:

cd /root/aws-docker-project

Check changes:

git status

Add changes:

git add .

Commit:

git commit -m "Update project"

Push:

git push

COMMON ERROR: NOT A GIT REPOSITORY

Error:

fatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories): .git

Cause:

You are not inside a Git repository.

Solution:

cd /root/aws-docker-project

Then:

git status

If Git has not been initialized:

git init

COMMON ERROR: REMOTE ORIGIN ALREADY EXISTS

Error:

error: remote origin already exists.

Solution:

Check:

git remote -v

Change URL:

git remote set-url origin git@github.com:ksumathi-senthil/aws-docker-project.git

COMMON ERROR: HTTPS 403

Error:

remote: Permission to ... denied
fatal: unable to access ... 403

Cause:

GitHub does not accept a normal GitHub password for Git HTTPS authentication.

Recommended solution:

Use SSH.

Set SSH remote:

git remote set-url origin git@github.com:ksumathi-senthil/aws-docker-project.git

COMMON ERROR: PERMISSION DENIED PUBLICKEY

Error:

git@github.com: Permission denied (publickey).

Cause:

GitHub cannot authenticate the EC2 SSH key.

Check:

ssh -T git@github.com

Test the specific key:

ssh -i /root/github_ed25519 -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -T git@github.com

Check the key exists:

ls -l /root/github_ed25519*

SSH KEY SECURITY

PRIVATE KEY:

/root/github_ed25519

KEEP THIS SECRET.

PUBLIC KEY:

/root/github_ed25519.pub

This can be added to GitHub.

NEVER COMMIT:

Private SSH keys
AWS access keys
AWS secret keys
Passwords
API keys
.env files
.pem files
Database credentials

FINAL VERIFICATION

Go to project:

cd /root/aws-docker-project

Check status:

git status

Check branch:

git branch

Check remote:

git remote -v

Test SSH:

ssh -T git@github.com

Push:

git push

